Checks (flag and, where fixable by light editing, correct in place):
- (a) The customer never uses root-cause language they could not know.
- (b) The agent's diagnostic path is plausible given only the symptoms in the messages.
- (c) Information ping-pong is appropriate to complexity: `simple` problems may resolve in 2–3 turns; `medium`/`complex` should show progressive discovery. Do NOT penalise a short conversation for a simple problem.
- (d) Tone is consistent with {{ inputs.customer_tone }} across all customer turns.
- (e) No contradictions between turns.
- (f) `end_reason` and the implied resolution match what the conversation actually achieved.
- (l) Identifiers, dates and history stated in the dialogue match the case record and the background: the machine model, serial number, site and company, and past events such as moves, changes or maintenance. Leaving a fact unsaid is fine; a stated detail that contradicts the record is not. Prefer correcting it by editing the turn.
{%- if inputs.diagnosis %}
- (m) Diagnosis lineage: the agent names a cause only after the customer has reported the finding that points to it, asks for the checks roughly in the plan's order, and stays within this contact's planned checks. The agent must not know or state the root cause before the evidence is in.
- (n) The customer's reported results match the plan's findings (reworded is fine; a different result is not), and the customer never reports a result for a check the agent did not ask for.
{%- endif %}
- (o) Report in `problem_state` the state the conversation ACTUALLY leaves the problem in, judged only from the text: `fixed_verified` (fixed, and the customer confirmed it works), `fixed_unverified` (fix applied, not yet confirmed), `workaround`, `pending_visit`, `pending_part`, `pending_customer_test` (the customer will run a check and report back), `escalated_open`, `not_a_fault`, or `abandoned` (the customer gave up); null if the contact was cut off before any of these. A booked technician or an ordered part is NOT fixed. Do not copy the planned state if the text does not reach it; if it misses the plan in a way light editing can fix, edit the turns and report the state of the edited text.
- (p) Every recorded commitment matches a promise in the text (who, what, when), and every explicit promise in the text (a visit, a callback, a part shipment, a test the customer will run) is recorded on the turn that makes it. Fix the recorded commitments in `edited_turns` where needed.
{%- if inputs.channel == "phone" %}
This is a phone-call transcript: `subject` is the logged reason for the call, turn 1 is the agent's scripted greeting, and `body` is the caller's first spoken turn. Turn 1 is a fixed script whose agent name is a placeholder: never edit it or flag it. Also check:
- (g) Every turn reads as speech, not writing: no email greetings or sign-offs, lists, or URLs read out. The agent cannot see the caller's machine and never claims to.
- (h) Bracketed tags are limited to `[hold]` (only at the start of an agent turn, after the caller agreed to hold), `[pause]`, and `[inaudible]`; `--` marks a cut-off. Remove any other tags, timestamps, or speaker labels inside turns.
- (i) Filler words, restarts, and self-corrections are intentional. Keep them; do not tidy the speech into written prose.
{%- endif %}
